  "scope": "Complete selected local reusable application foundation and scoped change-assessment capability; unchanged REQ-033 AC-1–8. This records candidate/real-case acceptance before the final status annotation transaction; final export/backup/outcome parity is separately recorded.",
  "implementationCommit": "7b25f0e011f150384cd85908003392e29023045b",
  "requirement": {
    "id": "db541d2d-b5e6-40a0-844d-771e737a9bd5",
    "version": 5,
    "acceptance": "AC-1: Versioned project-model bindings define application vocabulary/roles and validate against the governing schema; incompatible or silently changed semantics cannot reinterpret an existing assessment. AC-2: Assessment scope retains baseline, root, selected directions/kinds/depth, exact candidate population, reasons, native/evidence basis and explicit completeness/work bounds. AC-3: Candidate dispositions retain rationale, action obligations and supporting basis; structural links, outcomes, drift and applicability remain independent. AC-4: Shared guarded operations enforce state transitions/cross-record consistency atomically and recover exact success after interruption/reopen without implicit retry. AC-5: Effective scoped completion requires the complete declared population and configured exceptions/actions to be accounted for and an immutable exact-input conclusion; a manually edited status string cannot establish completion or release approval. AC-6: Later source/model/population changes retain historical conclusions and expose current applicability/uncertainty; bounded paged reads do not imply complete impact discovery. AC-7: A real Tracer source change and a materially different configured domain exercise the same headless operations, including invalid, stale, partial and reopened cases. AC-8: Shared UI authoring/assessment uses the same semantics, retains drafts/recovery and explains outstanding work; agent comprehension is distinguished from human validation.",
    "scopeNote": "Reusable application layer and scoped change-assessment proof over existing primitives; no generic rules engine or universal industry lifecycle."
  },
